WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.490
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:12.390
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I'm in denial.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:16.700
I don't want to face the reality that
my time at the GSB is coming to an end.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:21.150
But that's what's happened.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:22.900
That's the harsh reality.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.530
And I have to decide,
where am I going to go next?

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:27.969
Perhaps some of you can relate.

00:00:29.516 --> 00:00:33.420
I was going to go back to the Midwest,
it's where I'm from originally.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:37.100
I grew up on a farm in Nebraska,
enjoyed playing outside,

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.890
had a tight knit community
that supported me.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:43.770
I even got to exhibit pigs at the county
fair in the summer, what a life.

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:51.150
And later I moved to Minnesota for work,
and I fell in love with the culture,

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.350
people that are so polite,
kind, and reserved.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.850
Even have a special term for it, it's
called Minnesota nice, it's a real thing.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.370
But believe it or not,

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:06.495
the Midwest actually hasn't been
a popular destination for GSB alums.

00:01:06.495 --> 00:01:10.440
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now I have been working on my

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.910
art skills, and for an illustration here,

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:15.698
I've created an exact
replica of the United States.

00:01:15.698 --> 00:01:17.396
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:01:17.396 --> 00:01:20.719
I looked back at where last
year's class ended up.

00:01:20.719 --> 00:01:22.974
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Right there.

00:01:22.974 --> 00:01:25.960
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You can see the distribution.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.708
We have quite a few in Silicon Valley and
New York,

00:01:29.708 --> 00:01:33.546
maybe even a few cowboys and
cowgirls in Texas, but

00:01:33.546 --> 00:01:38.563
a huge hole in between,
only about 2% were going to the Midwest.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.084
But I kept hope.

00:01:42.084 --> 00:01:43.970
Maybe our class would be different.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:47.091
So I sent a survey to my classmates.

00:01:47.091 --> 00:01:53.890
60 responded, and of that 60, 0 were
going to the Midwest after graduation.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:01.620
Common reasons included things like it's
too cold, the culture is too conservative,

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:05.250
there's nothing to do from a social or
professional aspect, and

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.170
I don't have ties to the region,
so why should I go?

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:11.210
I started to reflect.

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:12.850
I mean, was I missing something here?

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.480
Should I not be going to the Midwest?

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.940
Perhaps people were missing out on
an opportunity by not even considering it.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:27.056
So I decided to take a fresh look at the
region in terms of career opportunities,

00:02:27.056 --> 00:02:30.901
quality of life, and
potential for societal impact.

00:02:32.631 --> 00:02:38.361
First from a career standpoint, I know
we all have extremely high aspirations,

00:02:38.361 --> 00:02:41.477
so I looked at what past alums have done,
and

00:02:41.477 --> 00:02:45.630
what they did to become
the most successful.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:49.000
For instance,
Phil Knight didn't stay in Silicon Valley.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.900
He went to Beaverton,
Oregon to build Nike.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:56.782
Mary Barra, she went to Detroit
to work for General Motors,

00:02:56.782 --> 00:03:02.246
eventually becoming CEO and
Fortune's most powerful woman in business.

00:03:02.246 --> 00:03:05.910
So perhaps there is a benefit to going
somewhere where everyone else isn't.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:14.540
For further research I spoke with Robert
Hadda, he's a partner at Drive Capital,

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:18.441
it's a VC firm focused on
funding Midwest startups.

00:03:18.441 --> 00:03:22.128
It was founded by two former Sequoia
partners who left Silicon Valley for

00:03:22.128 --> 00:03:26.610
Columbus, Ohio, of all places, because of
the opportunities they saw in the region.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.780
Part of that is, it's a very big economy.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:35.010
You have a quarter the Fortune
500 companies in the region.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:39.600
So for those GSB students who want to
work for a large company right away,

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:43.710
there's ample opportunities in
broad industries that truly matter.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:48.150
Things like healthcare,
financial services, food and agriculture.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:53.979
For those who want to do something
maybe a little more exciting,

00:03:53.979 --> 00:03:56.355
there's even a budding startup scene.

00:03:56.355 --> 00:04:00.244
One of the benefits of being the region
as a startup is you're close to many

00:04:00.244 --> 00:04:02.940
of your end consumers and competitors.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:05.560
So you're able to respond, and
learn from them more quickly.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:10.440
There's also a lot of top
state schools in the region.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:14.929
So you're able to draw from top talent
in computer science and engineering,

00:04:14.929 --> 00:04:18.530
without competing with companies
like Facebook and Google.

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:22.129
Specifically as GSBs, we have
the opportunity to take some of the best

00:04:22.129 --> 00:04:25.240
practices that we learned at Stanford and
Silicon Valley and

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:27.320
bring them to the region to help grow it.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:33.900
Things like rapid product development,
and scaling, building world class teams,

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.390
that because of current technology
you can do from anywhere.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:38.760
So, who knows?

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:41.560
Maybe we can even apply some of
those touchy feely skills and

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:43.890
we'll build the first corn
unicorn in the region.

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:44.398
Sounds great.

00:04:44.398 --> 00:04:49.810
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I also spoke with David Kit.

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:53.390
He lived in Chicago and Minneapolis and
was my alumni interviewer.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.690
He worked in finance and e-commerce.

00:04:56.690 --> 00:05:01.250
And he pointed out that being somewhere
where there's a relatively smaller network

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:02.990
is actually powerful.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:05.698
Those who are in the region become closer.

00:05:05.698 --> 00:05:07.150
And you can leverage that network more.

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:12.310
As an employee,
you become more powerful to your employer.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:16.180
Because you might be one of the few who
can tap into the broader Stanford network.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:17.390
It's a good situation to be in.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:23.270
So outside just the career aspects though,
there is quality of life to consider.

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:26.720
Fast forward, say, five years.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:30.440
You're ready to settle down,
married, perhaps have a kid.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:31.840
Hopefully you've even
made your first million.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:38.195
Well, maybe you want to buy a house with
that, but if you're in San Francisco-

00:05:38.195 --> 00:05:40.069
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:40.069 --> 00:05:41.998
&gt;&gt; Something like that,

00:05:41.998 --> 00:05:44.160
kind of a fixer-upper.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:46.810
Instead, say you decide
to go to Minneapolis.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:49.628
You end up with something like this.

00:05:49.628 --> 00:05:52.935
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Prime lakefront property.

00:05:52.935 --> 00:05:55.293
Your choice.

00:05:55.293 --> 00:05:58.685
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now,

00:05:58.685 --> 00:06:02.010
it's not just the housing that's awesome.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:06.100
Recently did a study looking in terms of
overall quality of life from the aspects

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:10.740
of affordability, economic opportunity,
and work life balance.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:14.690
All five of the top cities in
its study were from the Midwest.

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:19.120
Places like Saint Paul, Minnesota,
Lincoln, Nebraska and Madison, Wisconsin.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:24.600
There is a benefit, too, of going
somewhere where there is slightly fewer

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:27.783
people, because it makes
activities more accessible to you.

00:06:27.783 --> 00:06:32.330
If you want to get reservations to your
favorite restaurant, say, it's easier.

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:34.930
Same with concert tickets and
sporting events.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:38.590
It's a great time to be in
the region as well for sports.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:42.942
I mean, you have the World Series
champion Chicago Cubs, and as knows,

00:06:42.942 --> 00:06:45.130
the NBA champion Cleveland Cavaliers.

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:47.700
Apologies to the Golden State fans,

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:49.660
I realize you're a little
sad your reign's over.

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:50.605
But it's okay.

00:06:50.605 --> 00:06:53.570
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I know we also had

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:54.570
a few foodies in the room.

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:58.330
There's tons of farms in the area,
in case you didn't know.

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:00.830
So you can eat farm fresh and
local, constantly.

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:02.860
And trust me,

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:07.420
if you're a carnivore like me, there's
no better place in the world for steaks.

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:07.920
I love it.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.529
But it's not just about the food.

00:07:11.529 --> 00:07:16.920
The thing I miss most, not being
in the region now, is the people.

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:21.568
I had the honor of having one of
my classmates, Michael, visit,

00:07:21.568 --> 00:07:24.283
he's Nigerian, over winter break.

00:07:24.283 --> 00:07:29.099
The thing that surprised him most was how
open and welcoming the people were in

00:07:29.099 --> 00:07:32.969
the region, something
Midwesterners pride themselves on.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.870
You say hi and wave to strangers.

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:39.349
It's kind of strange at first,
but you get used to it.

00:07:39.349 --> 00:07:42.762
If you haven't visited, I suggest you
do and you'll notice the difference.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:49.643
Now, in terms of societal impact,
collectively,

00:07:49.643 --> 00:07:52.578
I do think our decisions matter.

00:07:52.578 --> 00:07:54.272
A popular topic at the moment for

00:07:54.272 --> 00:07:58.000
instance is the divide that's
developed in our country.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.638
Much of that is geographic based,
the coasts and Middle America.

00:08:03.947 --> 00:08:07.538
Well, call me crazy, but I don't
think that's going away anytime soon,

00:08:07.538 --> 00:08:09.771
if we just stay in Silicon Valley and
New York.

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:14.170
If you want to see a change in a region,
why not move there?

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:15.600
Bring it.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:17.630
If you think people have been left behind,

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:19.920
try to bring them new
economic opportunities.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:23.510
I realize there might be some resistance,

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:26.950
being like, these people are different
from me, I don't know how to connect.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:30.920
But from my experience in
living in both regions,

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:32.729
there's more similarities
than differences.

00:08:33.790 --> 00:08:37.000
It's also enjoyable to
hear a variety of thought.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:39.510
That's how you truly learn to
understand one another and

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:40.760
find common ground to connect.

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:45.140
After talking through and

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:50.360
reflecting on my decision, I am confident
that I do want to go to the Midwest.

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:54.871
If you're still deciding and
want to be able stand out in your career,

00:08:54.871 --> 00:09:00.242
have an exceptional quality of life, and
help to unify our country, then join me.

00:09:00.242 --> 00:09:02.447
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:02.447 --> 00:09:06.659
[MUSIC]

